1
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,480
Let's get started for today

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,960
Let's start with a recap of what we covered last time

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,920
because we're going to be building very heavily

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:16,120
on Wednesday's concepts

5
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,480
So we talked about online reinforcement

6
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,600
learning where we collect a batch of data with some policy

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,840
by running that policy in the environment

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,440
and then we try to use that data to improve our policy

9
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:33,560
and then we repeat this process in order to continue

10
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,300
to improve from practice

11
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,720
We talked about an online reinforcement learning algorithm

12
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,640
called policy gradients

13
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,040
And the update for our policy looks something

14
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:49,060
like this, where we first take samples from our policy

15
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,600
This will give us N different trajectories

16
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:53,880
sampled from our policy

17
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,460
We will evaluate the log likelihood of the policy

18
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,980
And so this pi here is representing a distribution

19
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:02,160
over actions given states

20
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:03,900
And so this is looking like the gradient

21
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:09,380
of the log probability of the actions under the policy

22
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:13,380
And this log likelihood is weighted by the reward

23
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,240
that the agent got

24
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,940
And so if you have higher rewards

25
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:20,580
as an outcome of your actions in the future

26
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:23,020
then you're going to be encouraging the policy

27
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,620
to increase the likelihood of those actions

28
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:30,660
And then lastly, we introduced a baseline

29
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,100
which we could define to be the average reward, such

30
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,220
that we're increasing the likelihood on futures that

31
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:39,020
were above average reward and decreasing

32
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:43,740
the likelihood on actions that led to futures

33
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:47,180
that were below average reward

34
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:50,500
So you can think of this as encouraging the policy

35
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:52,360
to do more of the above average stuff

36
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,100
less of the below average stuff, and then

37
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:59,290
repeating that process to get better and better

38
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:00,690
Cool

39
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:04,530
We also talked about how this algorithm is fully on-policy

40
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:08,370
So in order to evaluate this, we actually

41
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:12,070
need to have samples from pi theta from our current policy

42
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:13,450
And this meant that we could only

43
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:17,050
take one gradient step per batch of data, which is disappointing

44
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:19,610
because then we'd have to collect ton of data

45
00:02:19,610 --> 00:02:21,370
in order to improve

46
00:02:21,370 --> 00:02:23,690
This brings up this notion of having on-policy

47
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,610
versus off-policy algorithms that can reuse data

48
00:02:26,610 --> 00:02:29,050
from other past policies

49
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:34,690
And we started to look at ways to make an algorithm off-policy

50
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:37,730
And in particular, we talked about using importance weights

51
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:41,930
where if we weight the objective by the probability

52
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:45,170
of the actions of the new policy divided

53
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:48,130
by the probability of the actions under the old policy

54
00:02:48,130 --> 00:02:52,090
this is a way to get a estimate of the gradient

55
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:56,370
for the new policy

56
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:59,270
I should also mention importance weights

57
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,510
The math behind it is very simple and very nice

58
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:05,990
But it's important to remember that this isn't a silver bullet

59
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:09,430
This approach is only suitable when the policies are

60
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:11,110
very similar to one another

61
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:12,930
And if the policies are very different

62
00:03:12,930 --> 00:03:16,390
then this ratio will get very large or very small

63
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:22,390
and you'll also just have a worse estimate of your gradient

64
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:24,310
So today we're going to be talking

65
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:27,830
about a form of algorithms called actor-critic methods that

66
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:32,220
build on policy gradients

67
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:33,590
As part of this, we'll be talking

68
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:35,830
about how to estimate the value of a policy

69
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:38,830
and then we'll introduce an off-policy variant

70
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:40,950
of the algorithm

71
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:44,710
And these actor-critic methods are the basis

72
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:47,270
for algorithms like PPO, which are

73
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,150
used to train language models, and that

74
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:57,130
are used to train humanoids and simulation and so forth

75
00:03:57,130 --> 00:04:00,700
So very, very useful RL algorithms

76
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,410
And then the key learning goals for today

77
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:05,330
is how to estimate how good a state and action is

78
00:04:05,330 --> 00:04:06,850
for a policy

79
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,410
This is called policy evaluation

80
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:12,770
It's a critical aspect of actor-critic methods

81
00:04:12,770 --> 00:04:14,690
but also useful more broadly

82
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:16,290
And then also talk about how to use

83
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:18,850
those estimates to form a better reinforcement learning

84
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:24,370
algorithm

85
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:25,650
So let's get started

86
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:27,590
And to talk about these algorithms

87
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:32,370
we need to revisit some very useful objects that we talked

88
00:04:32,370 --> 00:04:35,010
about in the first lecture, and specifically, this

89
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:36,970
is value functions

90
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:41,090
And so a value function is representing

91
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:45,490
how good a state is if you're going

92
00:04:45,490 --> 00:04:48,610
to follow a policy starting from that state

93
00:04:48,610 --> 00:04:51,290
And you can think of it as the sum

94
00:04:51,290 --> 00:04:55,830
of rewards used to writing gradients

95
00:04:55,830 --> 00:04:57,690
starting from some state

96
00:04:57,690 --> 00:05:02,100
So we'll write this as V pi of S

97
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:04,310
This means that we're going to be starting at state S

98
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:06,070
and then running pi

99
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:14,290
And for a given state, this is equal to the sum of rewards

100
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:22,670
if we were to start at state t and go

101
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:24,750
to the end of the trajectory

102
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:29,590
And here's the sum of rewards

103
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:33,870
Now, this sum of rewards is going

104
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:37,510
to differ depending on what state we start at

105
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:39,730
and depending on what policy that we're running

106
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:49,590
And so this is going to be an expectation under our policy

107
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:52,940
Actually maybe it's easier to write this as expectation

108
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:53,520
on the inside

109
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:59,660
So this is an expectation over the states St prime

110
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:05,940
and actions at prime that will be visited under our policy

111
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:14,060
and conditioned on the fact that we're starting at state St

112
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:17,700
So we can think about the value function as this

113
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,900
Just in terms of math, what you can think about this as is--

114
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:24,420
or just in terms of conceptually or in terms of intuition

115
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:26,580
if you have some trajectory, and you're

116
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:30,500
thinking about the value of this state, you can think of it as--

117
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,140
if we were to collect a lot of different trajectories

118
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:36,740
starting from that state from our policy, and then

119
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:41,880
we just summed up the rewards from this time step to the end

120
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:43,980
and then averaged over these trajectories

121
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:47,140
that would define the value of this state

122
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:51,640
or how good the state is for the policy

123
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,200
Another useful quantity that will be making use of

124
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,450
is called the Q function

125
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:58,740
And this is slightly different from the value function

126
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:00,600
but very closely related

127
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:03,520
And the difference is that instead of just thinking

128
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,190
about the value of a given state

129
00:07:05,190 --> 00:07:07,320
we're going to be thinking about a value of a given

130
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,560
state and action

131
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:15,640
And what this is referring to is instead of starting at S

132
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:18,320
and then running pi from there, if instead we

133
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:21,500
were to start at S, take a particular action a

134
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:27,600
and then run the policy, what is our expected sum of rewards

135
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,760
And so then you can think of this as--

136
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:34,720
instead of a picture like this, say

137
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,120
we had some State s that we considered--

138
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,920
first we're going to take some given action

139
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:43,040
This may or may not have been what the policy would

140
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,160
have been likely to choose

141
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,330
This action could actually also lead to different states

142
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:54,500
because the environment is stochastic

143
00:07:54,500 --> 00:08:03,700
and then it's the sum of these rewards on average

144
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:06,380
So this is what V looks like

145
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:13,980
This is what Q looks like for a given action

146
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,120
So here are some pictures on the slide

147
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:19,060
So if we start from state s, run our policy

148
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:22,780
we compute the expected sum of the rewards

149
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:25,220
along these trajectories, then that

150
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:28,340
gets us the value of a state

151
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,740
And then if instead we take action a first and then run pi

152
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:36,900
then we get the Q values, and it's just the sum of the rewards

153
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:42,380
if we had started with action a

154
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:46,220
Now, one thing that's useful to think about in terms

155
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:47,820
of how these two relate to each other

156
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:51,840
is you can think of the value of a state as--

157
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,840
if you take an expectation over the actions

158
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,480
that the policy would have taken

159
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:59,080
you can think of it as the average or the expectation

160
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:01,400
of the Q values over all the actions

161
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:04,920
that the policy would have taken

162
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:09,240
And then one last object that we'll use today

163
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:13,440
is thinking about how good is a particular action

164
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:18,040
relative to other actions that we might take

165
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:22,520
And for this, we can think about

166
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:28,960
if you consider the Q value minus the value function, that's

167
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:31,040
going to tell you, how good is it to take action

168
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:35,000
a versus following the policy at a given state

169
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,520
And this is what is the advantage

170
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:42,720
of taking one action versus following the policy

171
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:43,300
Good question

172
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:52,550
So the question was, is there a normalization between V and Q

173
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:55,910
You can think of an expectation as an average

174
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:58,070
And so in that sense, there is going

175
00:09:58,070 --> 00:10:00,990
to be a normalization because if you were to

176
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:04,110
for example, try to estimate this expectation with samples

177
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:06,370
you would take some number of samples

178
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:08,830
If you only use these two samples to estimate Q

179
00:10:08,830 --> 00:10:10,810
then you would divide by 2, versus here

180
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:13,910
if you're using five samples, you would divide by 5

181
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:16,310
Is there a way that you can get from this equation

182
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:18,350
to the useful relation on the slide

183
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:20,810
Is that what you're asking

184
00:10:20,810 --> 00:10:28,790
Yeah

185
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:31,170
We can write out first what Q pi looks like

186
00:10:31,170 --> 00:10:40,410
So Q pi I of a given state and action

187
00:10:40,410 --> 00:10:44,710
We're going to condition on St, at because that's

188
00:10:44,710 --> 00:10:45,870
where we're starting

189
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:52,530
And then this is the expected sum of rewards if we--

190
00:10:52,530 --> 00:10:57,760
So a sum over t prime equals t to T

191
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:00,010
And then we're going to have an expectation of running

192
00:11:00,010 --> 00:11:12,690
the policy here, and then a sum of rewards

193
00:11:12,690 --> 00:11:24,230
And then in terms of seeing how to get between these two things

194
00:11:24,230 --> 00:11:27,290
you can think of this as a sum of rewards

195
00:11:27,290 --> 00:11:34,890
under at future actions sampled from pi

196
00:11:34,890 --> 00:11:38,550
whereas this one at is not sampled from pi

197
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:40,810
but all future actions are sampled from pi

198
00:11:40,810 --> 00:11:45,270
In this case, we're conditioning on the action

199
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:46,810
that's provided as input

200
00:11:46,810 --> 00:11:49,330
And in this case, we're sampling from the policy

201
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:56,110
And so if instead you sample the action from the policy

202
00:11:56,110 --> 00:12:05,190
and then feed that in to here, then you'll get this right here

203
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:08,670
So to help you start thinking about some of these quantities

204
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:10,550
let's look at an example

205
00:12:10,550 --> 00:12:15,110
And so say that you are this person right here

206
00:12:15,110 --> 00:12:19,150
You really want to learn how to play the drums

207
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:26,510
And your reward is 1 if you can play the music in a month, and 0

208
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:27,210
otherwise

209
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:29,870
So capital T is a month from now

210
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:33,630
And you have three different possible actions

211
00:12:33,630 --> 00:12:34,950
in your action space

212
00:12:34,950 --> 00:12:37,510
One action is to sit on the beach

213
00:12:37,510 --> 00:12:40,950
Another action is to watch TV

214
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:45,740
And another action is to practice playing the drums

215
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,540
And say that your current policy right now is just

216
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:54,140
to sit on the beach, and you do that for all states

217
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:56,660
Now, my question for you to think about

218
00:12:56,660 --> 00:13:02,680
is, what are each of these quantities for the action of a1

219
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:05,640
for the action of a2, and for the action of a3

220
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:09,210
And so specifically you want to think about

221
00:13:09,210 --> 00:13:11,780
what is the value function of your current state

222
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:15,100
for this policy

223
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:18,420
What is your Q function if you were

224
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:22,380
to follow each of these actions once and then follow

225
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:23,660
this policy

226
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:29,020
And then what is your advantage function for the current state

227
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:33,060
that you're in and for each of these actions

228
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:36,500
And you can assume that in the current state that you're in

229
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:40,020
you do not know how to play the drums

230
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:42,730
What was described as for the value function

231
00:13:42,730 --> 00:13:44,400
for this particular policy that's always

232
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:47,000
sitting on the beach, the value is 0

233
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,100
because the person would not learn to play the drums

234
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:53,680
so that's the sum of expected rewards for this policy

235
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,640
for V pi

236
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:59,360
For the Q function, if you sit on the beach at the current time

237
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:06,920
step or watch TV, the Q function will be 0 for those actions

238
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:10,480
The Q function for playing the drums at the current time step

239
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:15,140
would be 1 if that amount of practicing is sufficient

240
00:14:15,140 --> 00:14:17,420
It's also possible that it would be a bit lower than 1

241
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:20,720
if you, for example, practice for an hour

242
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:23,380
and then sit on the beach by following the policy afterwards

243
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:29,160
it depends a little bit on how long a given time step is

244
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:33,020
And then the advantage function is going to be Q minus V

245
00:14:33,020 --> 00:14:35,520
and so it's going to be a positive number

246
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:37,640
for the action of practicing, and it'll

247
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:41,380
be 0 for the other two actions

248
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:44,260
So the question is, if practicing is not

249
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:47,000
enough to actually learn, would it be 1

250
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:48,240
Would it be between 0 and 1

251
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:50,540
Would it be 0

252
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:53,620
It basically actually represents the probability

253
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:56,380
in which you would be able to play this, given that you had

254
00:14:56,380 --> 00:14:57,820
practiced for that amount of time

255
00:14:57,820 --> 00:15:01,800
and that probability it could be 0

256
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:05,760
Maybe this is really, really hard after just practicing once

257
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,180
It could also be maybe for someone

258
00:15:07,180 --> 00:15:08,720
who's extremely talented

259
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:10,260
They only need to practice once

260
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:12,130
And so that could be a bit higher for someone

261
00:15:12,130 --> 00:15:14,140
that's a bit more talented

262
00:15:14,140 --> 00:15:17,860
It depends on various factors like that

263
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:20,860
Along those lines, if you think about the value function

264
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:24,180
if you're playing a game of chess, for example

265
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:26,260
the value function is the probability

266
00:15:26,260 --> 00:15:31,700
that you're going to win for a given person

267
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:33,000
and for a given game state

268
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,860
So it's evaluating how good is the state

269
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:38,120
for a particular person

270
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:43,860
That's another way to think about it

271
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:47,320
So those are the objects that we're going

272
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:49,480
to be using quite a bit today

273
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:52,200
Now let's go back to policy gradients

274
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:54,080
and see how we can use value functions

275
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,120
to make this algorithm better

276
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,360
So we talked about a couple examples

277
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:04,280
on Wednesday of some scenarios that we're dissatisfying

278
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:06,080
about policy gradients

279
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:10,160
And the first was that if we are trying to learn how to walk

280
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:12,560
and we have a policy that takes a small step forwards

281
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:16,400
and then falls backwards, we will push down

282
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:18,880
the likelihood of taking a step forward

283
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:20,960
because we don't necessarily know that might

284
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:23,440
have led it to fall backwards

285
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,120
Now, if we actually had some understanding

286
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:28,400
that taking a small step forward was making progress

287
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:31,080
was actually good, was advantageous

288
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:33,560
then we might be able to actually increase

289
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:35,280
the likelihood of that, and so this

290
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:37,270
is starting to hint at some of the intuition

291
00:16:37,270 --> 00:16:40,870
behind actor-critic methods

292
00:16:40,870 --> 00:16:43,510
And likewise, we talked about this example

293
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:45,390
of trying to fold a jacket

294
00:16:45,390 --> 00:16:49,990
And we found that if you have a sparse reward

295
00:16:49,990 --> 00:16:52,670
and you only do part of the task by folding only the sleeves

296
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:55,470
or flattening the jacket but not folding it

297
00:16:55,470 --> 00:16:58,910
you aren't really utilizing the trajectories that

298
00:16:58,910 --> 00:17:01,910
are getting zero reward

299
00:17:01,910 --> 00:17:06,150
And similar to this case, if we instead

300
00:17:06,150 --> 00:17:08,750
understood that these actions are advantageous for making

301
00:17:08,750 --> 00:17:10,750
progress on the task, then we might actually

302
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:13,470
be able to figure out how to use these trajectories

303
00:17:13,470 --> 00:17:15,349
in a positive way

304
00:17:15,349 --> 00:17:18,270
And so to summarize here, policy gradients

305
00:17:18,270 --> 00:17:20,630
don't always make an efficient use of data

306
00:17:20,630 --> 00:17:22,030
that we've collected

307
00:17:22,030 --> 00:17:25,970
And if we can try to figure out what actions are advantageous

308
00:17:25,970 --> 00:17:27,349
versus not advantageous, we might

309
00:17:27,349 --> 00:17:30,870
be able to make better use of that data

310
00:17:30,870 --> 00:17:36,230
So the question was, if a step forward is helpful or not

311
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:41,250
is that going to be negative or neutral

312
00:17:41,250 --> 00:17:46,090
In case of this policy gradient algorithm, the trajectory

313
00:17:46,090 --> 00:17:48,210
that we considered here in the context of the batch

314
00:17:48,210 --> 00:17:52,130
of trajectories, net negative was below average

315
00:17:52,130 --> 00:17:55,230
because the reward to go is quite negative

316
00:17:55,230 --> 00:18:01,330
It's actually making backwards progress in this trajectory

317
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:03,410
And so it actually is going to be pushing down

318
00:18:03,410 --> 00:18:05,970
the likelihood of taking a step forward

319
00:18:05,970 --> 00:18:09,810
because the future reward to go is negative

320
00:18:09,810 --> 00:18:12,370
And what we'll talk about today is

321
00:18:12,370 --> 00:18:14,490
algorithms that in principle could actually

322
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:16,850
increase the likelihood for taking the step forward

323
00:18:16,850 --> 00:18:23,250
and decrease the likelihood for falling backwards

324
00:18:23,250 --> 00:18:26,570
So how do we do that

325
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:30,590
A lot of what we'll focus on today is this piece right here

326
00:18:30,590 --> 00:18:32,510
This is what's actually telling us

327
00:18:32,510 --> 00:18:34,590
that we should push down the likelihood if we have

328
00:18:34,590 --> 00:18:38,710
negative rewards in the future

329
00:18:38,710 --> 00:18:41,550
And this quantity, it's estimating the future

330
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:47,510
rewards if we take action a in state s

