00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:38,140
the rewards that we saw, which was, in the case of the example

347
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:39,980
before, the rewards were negative

348
00:19:39,980 --> 00:19:43,820
because we took a small step forward and then fell backwards

349
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:48,580
But if instead what we could do is actually run the policy

350
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:54,460
many, many times and get a Q function for the state

351
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:56,300
that would be the true expected rewards

352
00:19:56,300 --> 00:19:59,700
to go under the expectation of our policy

353
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:01,640
and this would be way better

354
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:06,740
We'd be able to get a much better sense for how good it

355
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:12,900
was to take that action in that state

356
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:17,180
So essentially the form of the gradient

357
00:20:17,180 --> 00:20:19,320
would be able to get a much better gradient

358
00:20:19,320 --> 00:20:20,740
if the form of the gradient looked

359
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:24,700
like this, where we increase the likelihood of the actions

360
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:26,450
for things that have high Q values

361
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:28,780
and we decrease the likelihood of the actions for things

362
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:33,760
that have low Q values

363
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:40,280
So that's a first step

364
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:42,840
We also talked about baselines in the previous lecture

365
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:46,840
where the baseline was the average reward

