we have Q. Q can be written as the expected future rewards

532
00:31:26,830 --> 00:31:28,450
which is the reward at the current time

533
00:31:28,450 --> 00:31:30,110
step plus the future rewards

534
00:31:30,110 --> 00:31:33,570
These future rewards can be written as the value

535
00:31:33,570 --> 00:31:36,290
And so we can think of Q as the reward at the current time

536
00:31:36,290 --> 00:31:43,370
step plus the value at the next time step

537
00:31:43,370 --> 00:31:48,610
So why is this an approximation

538
00:31:48,610 --> 00:31:52,410
The reason for this is that there's actually

539
00:31:52,410 --> 00:31:54,450
multiple possible next states

540
00:31:54,450 --> 00:31:59,190
And we're just going to use one sample from this expectation

541
00:31:59,190 --> 00:32:01,730
which is the sample that we saw when we rolled out

542
00:32:01,730 --> 00:32:04,010
the trajectory in our data

543
00:32:04,010 --> 00:32:06,050
And if you want an exact thing, you

544
00:32:06,050 --> 00:32:08,050
would need to estimate this expectation

545
00:32:08,050 --> 00:32:10,050
with infinite samples

546
00:32:10,050 --> 00:32:11,750
How good of an approximation is this

547
00:32:11,750 --> 00:32:15,630
So it is going to be a single sample approximation

548
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:19,230
The good news is that later on we'll

549
00:32:19,230 --> 00:32:27,170
see ways to use lots of data to do this

550
00:32:27,170 --> 00:32:28,630
Well, so I guess the first thing is

551
00:32:28,630 --> 00:32:30,980
that if this is very deterministic

552
00:32:30,980 --> 00:32:33,190
first, it's going to be a good estimate because there

553
00:32:33,190 --> 00:32:35,290
aren't that many futures

554
00:32:35,290 --> 00:32:36,710
If it's more stochastic, then it's

555
00:32:36,710 --> 00:32:38,950
going to be a worse estimate, and then we'll

556
00:32:38,950 --> 00:32:41,110
see ways in which we can use data and take

557
00:32:41,110 --> 00:32:44,950
lots of gradient steps in a way that makes it not too bad

558
00:32:44,950 --> 00:32:47,510
So the question is, is there any new interaction

559
00:32:47,510 --> 00:32:48,650
with the environment

560
00:32:48,650 --> 00:32:53,150
So I guess we haven't yet talked about how to fit V pi

561
00:32:53,150 --> 00:32:56,470
So we will talk about that in the next slide

562
00:32:56,470 --> 00:33:00,790
So if you have some trajectory this

563
00:33:00,790 --> 00:33:05,470
will be like the St, at and St plus 1 from the same trajectory

564
00:33:05,470 --> 00:33:08,470
So you need to use the same trajectory for this

565
00:33:08,470 --> 00:33:12,810
And sometimes I forget to put the pi on here

566
00:33:12,810 --> 00:33:15,170
And there is a lot of reinforcement learning work that

567
00:33:15,170 --> 00:33:17,310
drops the pis just for simplicity

568
00:33:17,310 --> 00:33:19,580
but it is actually, in some ways

569
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:21,410
very important to have A pi there because it

570
00:33:21,410 --> 00:33:23,930
is estimating a value for a particular policy

571
00:33:23,930 --> 00:33:25,720
not for any policy

572
00:33:25,720 --> 00:33:27,850
So the question is, why are we choosing to estimate

573
00:33:27,850 --> 00:33:30,290
V versus Q versus A

574
00:33:30,290 --> 00:33:33,090
It's just a little bit simpler in practice

575
00:33:33,090 --> 00:33:38,970
But there are also ways to directly estimate Q

576
00:33:38,970 --> 00:33:42,050
And actually we'll talk about some of those ways next week

577
00:33:42,050 --> 00:33:49,690
as well

578
00:33:49,690 --> 00:33:51,450
So we're just going to fit V pi

579
00:33:51,450 --> 00:33:53,630
And then once we fit V pi we can get the advantage

580
00:33:53,630 --> 00:33:58,450
and then plug that into our gradient for our policy

581
00:33:58,450 --> 00:34:01,190
We can represent V pi as a neural network

582
00:34:01,190 --> 00:34:02,730
And what this will look like is we'll

583
00:34:02,730 --> 00:34:06,250
pass as input a state and then it

584
00:34:06,250 --> 00:34:11,210
will output a single scalar value

585
00:34:11,210 --> 00:34:14,960
that represents the value of that state for that policy

586
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:18,639
This neural network will have some parameters

587
00:34:18,639 --> 00:34:20,159
So how do we do this

588
00:34:20,159 --> 00:34:31,920
So there's a few different ways to estimate V pi

589
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:33,800
So in the very, very initial version

590
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:35,639
like a vanilla policy gradient, we just

591
00:34:35,639 --> 00:34:39,080
used a single trajectory, a single sample

592
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:42,520
And instead of actually learning a model

593
00:34:42,520 --> 00:34:46,440
we just used that sample

594
00:34:46,440 --> 00:34:56,020
In principle, we could also use many samples

595
00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:58,960
Unfortunately, if you were to try to go to a particular state

596
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:01,040
and then take many samples from that state

597
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:02,540
that means that you would have to be

598
00:35:02,540 --> 00:35:04,720
able to reset the world to that state

599
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:10,020
and see what happens if you took many different rollouts

600
00:35:10,020 --> 00:35:12,100
from that same state

601
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:13,920
And unfortunately, in many scenarios

602
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:16,260
it's very hard to reset the state

603
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:19,320
If you are in simulated settings

604
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,830
sometimes you can actually reset the simulator

605
00:35:21,830 --> 00:35:24,080
So there is sometimes scenarios where you can do this

606
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:31,540
but in practice, we typically don't do this

607
00:35:31,540 --> 00:35:34,140
So since we can't directly reset and take

608
00:35:34,140 --> 00:35:37,060
lots of samples, what we can do is

609
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:39,740
we can use multiple trajectories that we had sampled

610
00:35:39,740 --> 00:35:43,660
and just train a single model on the original single sample

611
00:35:43,660 --> 00:35:44,860
estimate

612
00:35:44,860 --> 00:35:47,780
And because the trajectories that we have

613
00:35:47,780 --> 00:35:51,380
they will have some states in them that are similar

614
00:35:51,380 --> 00:36:00,960
You'll be able to essentially learn how to amortize the data

615
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:04,180
So you'll see some examples in the environment that

616
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:06,700
have very similar states and have

617
00:36:06,700 --> 00:36:11,080
corresponding future rewards in them

618
00:36:11,080 --> 00:36:14,160
And with that repetition in your data

619
00:36:14,160 --> 00:36:18,080
a neural network can have a little bit more density

620
00:36:18,080 --> 00:36:23,000
in terms of the rewards that you'll see for a given state

621
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:25,960
And so what this is going to look like is

622
00:36:25,960 --> 00:36:34,840
you can take your data set, and you'll

623
00:36:34,840 --> 00:36:40,320
have I equals 1 to n trajectories

624
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:44,640
And each trajectory will have time steps in it

625
00:36:44,640 --> 00:36:51,160
And for all of those trajectories and time steps

626
00:36:51,160 --> 00:36:53,640
you'll also have some sum of rewards

627
00:36:53,640 --> 00:36:56,640
that you observe for that

628
00:36:56,640 --> 00:36:59,560
So you'll have some sum of rewards

629
00:36:59,560 --> 00:37:11,910
that started at t to the end of the rewards of the states

630
00:37:11,910 --> 00:37:13,870
and actions that you visited

631
00:37:13,870 --> 00:37:17,150
And if you aggregate all the trajectories in your data set

632
00:37:17,150 --> 00:37:21,230
you can train a model to go from the state

633
00:37:21,230 --> 00:37:25,270
that you saw to the sum of rewards that was observed

634
00:37:25,270 --> 00:37:27,350
when you ran the policy

635
00:37:27,350 --> 00:37:30,990
And with this, you can form a supervised learning

636
00:37:30,990 --> 00:37:38,710
data set where this is the label in the data set

637
00:37:38,710 --> 00:37:40,310
And you can train with supervised

638
00:37:40,310 --> 00:37:43,510
learning a model to take as input this and output

639
00:37:43,510 --> 00:37:45,310
this quantity right here

640
00:37:45,310 --> 00:37:47,230
And the number of data points in your data set

641
00:37:47,230 --> 00:37:48,870
will be the number of trajectories

642
00:37:48,870 --> 00:37:51,870
times the average number of time steps

643
00:37:51,870 --> 00:37:54,210
So the question is, each time your policy changes

644
00:37:54,210 --> 00:37:58,220
do you have to recollect the data and refit this

645
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:00,510
With this approach, yes

646
00:38:00,510 --> 00:38:02,630
We will see approaches later

647
00:38:02,630 --> 00:38:05,690
This is the simplest and dumbest approach you can

648
00:38:05,690 --> 00:38:08,930
use to fit a value function

649
00:38:08,930 --> 00:38:12,870
And we will see approaches where you can reuse other data

650
00:38:12,870 --> 00:38:14,330
So the question was, if we're going

651
00:38:14,330 --> 00:38:16,410
to be running a lot of simulations

652
00:38:16,410 --> 00:38:19,290
or rollouts of our policy, is it unreasonable to try

653
00:38:19,290 --> 00:38:21,570
to learn some estimate of future states

654
00:38:21,570 --> 00:38:27,970
given the current state and action

655
00:38:27,970 --> 00:38:28,990
Two things here

656
00:38:28,990 --> 00:38:33,210
If you have a very simple state space, a very small state space

657
00:38:33,210 --> 00:38:36,930
you might be able to store some things

658
00:38:36,930 --> 00:38:39,560
and form an estimate just from storing things

659
00:38:39,560 --> 00:38:41,730
You could also, in principle, train a neural network

660
00:38:41,730 --> 00:38:44,450
to predict what future states would be given current states

661
00:38:44,450 --> 00:38:46,210
and actions

662
00:38:46,210 --> 00:38:48,950
There are a set of approaches that do that

663
00:38:48,950 --> 00:38:51,370
and they're called model-based approaches and that they're

664
00:38:51,370 --> 00:38:53,930
learning a model of the world

665
00:38:53,930 --> 00:39:00,970
And we will talk about those approaches in a few weeks

666
00:39:00,970 --> 00:39:07,850
So this first approach, run a bunch of samples

667
00:39:07,850 --> 00:39:12,270
aggregate a data set of our future rewards

668
00:39:12,270 --> 00:39:16,310
and then run supervised learning to fit an estimated value

669
00:39:16,310 --> 00:39:21,990
function where you can form an L2 error

670
00:39:21,990 --> 00:39:27,950
between your predicted value and the observed sum of rewards

671
00:39:27,950 --> 00:39:29,630
and then minimize that with respect

672
00:39:29,630 --> 00:39:36,030
to the parameters of your value function

673
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:41,550
And so what this objective looks like is minimizing

674
00:39:41,550 --> 00:39:44,310
with respect to your parameters the difference

675
00:39:44,310 --> 00:39:55,510
between your predicted value and the example

676
00:39:55,510 --> 00:39:57,030
or the sum of rewards that you saw

677
00:39:57,030 --> 00:40:01,310
in your data set for a given i

678
00:40:01,310 --> 00:40:06,250
And you'll do this for all of the data points

679
00:40:06,250 --> 00:40:09,010
in your data set

680
00:40:09,010 --> 00:40:11,490
And so this actually can also be a sum over i

681
00:40:11,490 --> 00:40:15,530
and t, just for completeness

682
00:40:15,530 --> 00:40:17,610
So that will be the objective

683
00:40:17,610 --> 00:40:19,690
And you can run gradient descent on this objective

684
00:40:19,690 --> 00:40:23,870
to fit a value function on your data point

685
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:26,370
Do we need to train a neural network for every gradient step

686
00:40:26,370 --> 00:40:27,230
in this case

687
00:40:27,230 --> 00:40:33,290
So this is actually different from policy gradient where--

688
00:40:33,290 --> 00:40:35,770
the gradient steps here that we take

689
00:40:35,770 --> 00:40:37,890
are going to be trying to fit a value function

690
00:40:37,890 --> 00:40:41,050
And the value function isn't changing the policy

691
00:40:41,050 --> 00:40:43,050
And so you can actually take many gradient steps

692
00:40:43,050 --> 00:40:47,250
on this objective to learn a value function without having

693
00:40:47,250 --> 00:40:51,930
to recollect your data

694
00:40:51,930 --> 00:40:54,750
So this is the simplest approach for training a value function

695
00:40:54,750 --> 00:40:57,010
It's called Monte Carlo estimation

696
00:40:57,010 --> 00:41:01,940
where essentially you're just running lots of simulations

697
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:05,320
running lots of rollouts, and then trying

698
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:11,440
to learn how to predict the outcome of the future rewards

699
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:14,920
Now, there's actually an approach

700
00:41:14,920 --> 00:41:18,400
that's better than this

701
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:23,320
And in particular, when we think about these sum of rewards

702
00:41:23,320 --> 00:41:25,620
maybe it's helpful to draw a picture in this case

703
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:35,920
So we have a bunch of trajectories that we've sampled

704
00:41:35,920 --> 00:41:44,160
And in this case, what we're doing is when we have a state

705
00:41:44,160 --> 00:41:51,040
right here, we are summing the rewards here as the value

706
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:55,600
And as we're learning this, we're

707
00:41:55,600 --> 00:42:01,820
starting to learn what the value of a given state is

708
00:42:01,820 --> 00:42:05,980
And this means that maybe halfway

709
00:42:05,980 --> 00:42:10,460
through training when we're regressing to future rewards--

710
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:14,180
and if we start to get what is a good value of this state right

711
00:42:14,180 --> 00:42:16,580
here, then we could actually try to use

712
00:42:16,580 --> 00:42:21,660
our own estimate of this value as the supervision target

713
00:42:21,660 --> 00:42:25,340
And in particular, if we know that we

714
00:42:25,340 --> 00:42:30,140
got a reward at this time step right here to then get to here

715
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:35,020
then we could actually use that reward plus our estimate

716
00:42:35,020 --> 00:42:39,740
as the supervision target rather than just using

717
00:42:39,740 --> 00:42:43,180
the sum of future rewards

718
00:42:43,180 --> 00:42:48,340
And so in particular, the ideal target

719
00:42:48,340 --> 00:42:52,260
is the true sum of expected rewards

720
00:42:52,260 --> 00:42:55,620
This is equal to the reward at the current time

721
00:42:55,620 --> 00:43:05,580
step plus the expected rewards at the next time step

722
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:07,120
The sum of rewards that the next time

723
00:43:07,120 --> 00:43:12,840
step is equal to the value of the next state

724
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:15,000
And so in principle, what we could

725
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:19,400
do is instead of trying to regress to future rewards

726
00:43:19,400 --> 00:43:24,640
we could regress to the reward at the current time

727
00:43:24,640 --> 00:43:30,480
step plus the value at the next time step

728
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:32,580
And so we're actually going to be, in this case

729
00:43:32,580 --> 00:43:36,520
using our estimate of the value at the next time step

730
00:43:36,520 --> 00:43:38,940
as part of the supervision target

731
00:43:38,940 --> 00:43:44,420
So these are all for a given trajectory

732
00:43:44,420 --> 00:43:55,160
and this would be your new label for your data set for a given

733
00:43:55,160 --> 00:43:57,140
state

734
00:43:57,140 --> 00:44:00,940
So the question is, how often do you update these V pi's

735
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:06,780
In practice, you can update them every single iteration

736
00:44:06,780 --> 00:44:08,600
If it's really expensive to update them

737
00:44:08,600 --> 00:44:10,640
you could update them less frequently if you have

738
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:12,100
a really huge neural network

739
00:44:12,100 --> 00:44:13,980
But in practice, typically, we update them

740
00:44:13,980 --> 00:44:17,260
every single gradient step

741
00:44:17,260 --> 00:44:19,620
So we have these bootstrapped estimates

742
00:44:19,620 --> 00:44:21,580
We refer to this as bootstrapping because we're

743
00:44:21,580 --> 00:44:24,260
actually bootstrapping off of our own estimate of the value

744
00:44:24,260 --> 00:44:28,700
function as part of the learning process

745
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:30,240
We formulate our training data

746
00:44:30,240 --> 00:44:32,500
We're then going to use the same exact objective

747
00:44:32,500 --> 00:44:33,960
to fit a value function

748
00:44:33,960 --> 00:44:35,440
Just regressing to these targets

749
00:44:35,440 --> 00:44:39,500
we just have different labels in our data set now

750
00:44:39,500 --> 00:44:47,100
And these labels are changing as we run gradient descent

751
00:44:47,100 --> 00:44:48,040
on our objective

752
00:44:48,040 --> 00:44:50,860
And so you often will update your labels

753
00:44:50,860 --> 00:44:53,490
every single gradient update

754
00:44:53,490 --> 00:44:55,620
So the question is, is this based on one trajectory

755
00:44:55,620 --> 00:44:59,210
or is this based on multiple trajectories

756
00:44:59,210 --> 00:45:02,010
where things are close together

757
00:45:02,010 --> 00:45:04,690
So this data set will be over multiple trajectories

758
00:45:04,690 --> 00:45:06,930
where i is indexing over trajectories

759
00:45:06,930 --> 00:45:11,480
and t is indexing over time inside those trajectories

760
00:45:11,480 --> 00:45:19,450
In terms of this label, this will be for a given data point

761
00:45:19,450 --> 00:45:22,370
And you're not going to be running multiple simulations

762
00:45:22,370 --> 00:45:24,650
from that data point, but you're going

763
00:45:24,650 --> 00:45:28,630
to be estimating what would have happened from your future state

764
00:45:28,630 --> 00:45:30,570
rather than a single sample of what

765
00:45:30,570 --> 00:45:32,510
happened in that trajectory

766
00:45:32,510 --> 00:45:33,890
And so with this, you're actually

767
00:45:33,890 --> 00:45:36,890
able to get a better estimate of all of the possible outcomes

768
00:45:36,890 --> 00:45:39,510
that might have happened at the next state

769
00:45:39,510 --> 00:45:41,810
rather than just one outcome

770
00:45:41,810 --> 00:45:45,490
So you're asking, why is the input to the value function

771
00:45:45,490 --> 00:45:50,490
just S and not including previous time steps

772
00:45:50,490 --> 00:45:58,510
Oh

773
00:45:58,510 --> 00:46:02,110
The ideal target for our value function

774
00:46:02,110 --> 00:46:03,950
is the sum of future rewards

775
00:46:03,950 --> 00:46:06,230
And you can think about this sum of future rewards

776
00:46:06,230 --> 00:46:09,910
as the reward at the current time step, which

777
00:46:09,910 --> 00:46:16,790
is a deterministic quantity plus the future rewards conditioned

778
00:46:16,790 --> 00:46:18,610
on our current state

779
00:46:18,610 --> 00:46:20,570
And so this is conditioned on the current state

780
00:46:20,570 --> 00:46:24,870
because the future rewards is a function of where we are right

781
00:46:24,870 --> 00:46:27,470
now, and it's actually only a function

782
00:46:27,470 --> 00:46:30,550
of where we are right now and not anything else

783
00:46:30,550 --> 00:46:32,670
So the question is, does this require that you have

784
00:46:32,670 --> 00:46:35,750
a good initial value estimate

785
00:46:35,750 --> 00:46:37,990
Because if it's really bad at the beginning

786
00:46:37,990 --> 00:46:42,390
then your targets will be really bad

787
00:46:42,390 --> 00:46:44,930
There are some considerations when initializing it

788
00:46:44,930 --> 00:46:46,670
although oftentimes you don't need

789
00:46:46,670 --> 00:46:49,130
to do anything super fancy to initialize it

790
00:46:49,130 --> 00:46:52,360
and you can just get started training on this directly

791
00:46:52,360 --> 00:46:54,730
You probably don't want to initialize your neural network

792
00:46:54,730 --> 00:46:58,570
such that the values are like negative 1000 or something, that

793
00:46:58,570 --> 00:46:59,670
would be bad

794
00:46:59,670 --> 00:47:02,170
But usually we don't initialize our neural networks that way

795
00:47:02,170 --> 00:47:07,020
anyway

796
00:47:07,020 --> 00:47:11,690
I don't know if I've in practice seen any issues with this

797
00:47:11,690 --> 00:47:14,290
But in principle, you could also start with a Monte Carlo

798
00:47:14,290 --> 00:47:16,410
objective just to get it in the right ballpark

799
00:47:16,410 --> 00:47:20,730
and then continue with this update after that

800
00:47:20,730 --> 00:47:22,430
So is this part of backpropagation

801
00:47:22,430 --> 00:47:24,370
and is there a better way to than updating

802
00:47:24,370 --> 00:47:25,870
the labels every step

803
00:47:25,870 --> 00:47:29,090
So we'll be using backpropagation

804
00:47:29,090 --> 00:47:31,370
to optimize that objective right there

805
00:47:31,370 --> 00:47:34,450
You could also think about this as propagating information

806
00:47:34,450 --> 00:47:38,890
backwards in time, which is distinct from propagating

807
00:47:38,890 --> 00:47:42,490
gradients through the network

808
00:47:42,490 --> 00:47:44,890
One other name for this is called temporal difference

809
00:47:44,890 --> 00:47:47,450
learning where we're looking at the difference

810
00:47:47,450 --> 00:47:53,540
between the future time step and the current time step

811
00:47:53,540 --> 00:47:56,960
But generally propagating information through time

812
00:47:56,960 --> 00:48:01,850
is fairly distinct from back propagation

813
00:48:01,850 --> 00:48:03,680
To summarize what we've talked about so far

814
00:48:03,680 --> 00:48:06,900
Monte Carlo were supervising with the rollout summed rewards

815
00:48:06,900 --> 00:48:08,920
whereas bootstrapping were supervising

816
00:48:08,920 --> 00:48:11,800
using the reward plus the current value

817
00:48:11,800 --> 00:48:15,040
estimate of the next state

818
00:48:15,040 --> 00:48:18,360
And if we look at an example--

819
00:48:18,360 --> 00:48:23,840
and actually, yeah, I'll give another exercise for you

820
00:48:23,840 --> 00:48:24,500
to figure out

821
00:48:24,500 --> 00:48:25,670
So let's look at an example

822
00:48:25,670 --> 00:48:27,400
So we run the policy

823
00:48:27,400 --> 00:48:28,900
Maybe we just have two trajectories

824
00:48:28,900 --> 00:48:30,480
In practice we'll have more than this

825
00:48:30,480 --> 00:48:32,520
But say we have two trajectories

826
00:48:32,520 --> 00:48:36,080
One of these trajectories the final reward was negative 1

827
00:48:36,080 --> 00:48:39,280
The other trajectory the final reward was one

828
00:48:39,280 --> 00:48:42,720
And along this directory, before the final state

829
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:45,800
the reward is 0

830
00:48:45,800 --> 00:48:51,540
My question for you is, what estimate

831
00:48:51,540 --> 00:48:55,260
would we get for two different states for the value function

832
00:48:55,260 --> 00:48:59,660
And in particular, if we were training on just these two

833
00:48:59,660 --> 00:49:02,740
trajectories, and we use Monte Carlo versus using

834
00:49:02,740 --> 00:49:05,380
temporal difference or bootstrap learning

835
00:49:05,380 --> 00:49:07,660
what would we estimate the value to be

836
00:49:07,660 --> 00:49:10,120
for the blue state for these two

837
00:49:10,120 --> 00:49:11,620
And what would we estimate the value

838
00:49:11,620 --> 00:49:14,980
to be for the pink state for these two

839
00:49:14,980 --> 00:49:17,580
So for Monte Carlo in the blue state

840
00:49:17,580 --> 00:49:20,580
it's going to average out the minus 1 and the 1

841
00:49:20,580 --> 00:49:25,300
and so this prediction will be 0

842
00:49:25,300 --> 00:49:28,440
Does anyone want to share another one

843
00:49:28,440 --> 00:49:28,940
Yeah

844
00:49:28,940 --> 00:49:30,700
So for the Monte Carlo for this one

845
00:49:30,700 --> 00:49:33,540
this will be negative 1 because it

846
00:49:33,540 --> 00:49:36,420
will be supervised to predict the sum of rewards

847
00:49:36,420 --> 00:49:39,540
for the future for this trajectory

848
00:49:39,540 --> 00:49:42,780
Anyone want to share

849
00:49:42,780 --> 00:49:44,540
Before we do that, let's talk about--

850
00:49:44,540 --> 00:49:46,140
do you want to do this one

851
00:49:46,140 --> 00:49:50,500
This is a little bit more intuitive to go this way

852
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:53,320
Yeah, so this will also be 0

853
00:49:53,320 --> 00:49:56,440
And then does anyone want to do the back part

854
00:49:56,440 --> 00:50:00,260
Yeah, so for this one, it'll see this blue one in the future

855
00:50:00,260 --> 00:50:03,000
This will have a 0, and then this will also

856
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:06,280
be assigned the hat of 0

857
00:50:06,280 --> 00:50:08,280
And so this is an example of where

858
00:50:08,280 --> 00:50:11,360
Monte Carlo and the bootstrap estimate

859
00:50:11,360 --> 00:50:12,880
are going to be different

860
00:50:12,880 --> 00:50:16,240
And arguably, we get a more accurate estimate

861
00:50:16,240 --> 00:50:19,320
from the bootstrap value because it's

862
00:50:19,320 --> 00:50:22,720
able to look at its estimate of the value

863
00:50:22,720 --> 00:50:25,480
and actually aggregate information across trajectories

864
00:50:25,480 --> 00:50:28,320
more effectively

865
00:50:28,320 --> 00:50:31,440
Why the Monte Carlo is minus 1 by the top right

866
00:50:31,440 --> 00:50:34,700
So the value for this one is going to be minus 1

867
00:50:34,700 --> 00:50:37,000
because the Monte Carlo objective is supervising

868
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:39,120
to the sum of future rewards that are

869
00:50:39,120 --> 00:50:40,660
observed for each trajectory

870
00:50:40,660 --> 00:50:43,020
And so for this trajectory, which is black

871
00:50:43,020 --> 00:50:47,840
it only sees the sum of future rewards along this trajectory

872
00:50:47,840 --> 00:50:49,620
So it only sees negative 1

873
00:50:49,620 --> 00:50:53,660
And anything over here, along this trajectory for Monte Carlo

874
00:50:53,660 --> 00:50:56,060
would have a target of one because it only

875
00:50:56,060 --> 00:50:59,100
sees the future trajectories here

876
00:50:59,100 --> 00:51:02,220
because it doesn't bootstrap off of its estimate that was

877
00:51:02,220 --> 00:51:04,420
using all of the trajectories

878
00:51:04,420 --> 00:51:07,620
So the Monte Carlo blue state, it's going to be--

879
00:51:07,620 --> 00:51:09,420
that exact state is in the buffer

880
00:51:09,420 --> 00:51:14,240
or is in your data set for both trajectories

881
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:17,940
And so it's going to average the reward for them

882
00:51:17,940 --> 00:51:19,500
But this is like the only scenario

883
00:51:19,500 --> 00:51:23,680
where Monte Carlo is going to get information

884
00:51:23,680 --> 00:51:25,300
from both trajectories because you have

885
00:51:25,300 --> 00:51:27,580
the same exact state in your data set

886
00:51:27,580 --> 00:51:29,700
with two different labels

887
00:51:29,700 --> 00:51:32,820
Why is the bootstrap blue state 0

888
00:51:32,820 --> 00:51:36,000
So this is also going to be averaging the future rewards

889
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:38,220
It doesn't have anything necessarily special

890
00:51:38,220 --> 00:51:40,020
to bootstrap off of

891
00:51:40,020 --> 00:51:44,300
And so for the same reason that Monte Carlo is 0

892
00:51:44,300 --> 00:51:46,770
this will also be V0

893
00:51:46,770 --> 00:51:49,250
So basically everything after the blue state

894
00:51:49,250 --> 00:51:52,110
will have a value estimate of negative 1 here

895
00:51:52,110 --> 00:51:55,490
And everything after the blue state on the block

896
00:51:55,490 --> 00:52:00,210
trajectory is going to have an estimate of negative ones

897
00:52:00,210 --> 00:52:03,730
We should move on

898
00:52:03,730 --> 00:52:09,850
So one question you might ask looking at this is--

899
00:52:09,850 --> 00:52:11,590
there might also be a middle ground

900
00:52:11,590 --> 00:52:16,290
So Monte Carlo is going to have high variance

901
00:52:16,290 --> 00:52:18,530
but it is completely unbiased

902
00:52:18,530 --> 00:52:22,570
Whereas the bootstrap estimate has much lower variance

903
00:52:22,570 --> 00:52:25,610
but it actually can be incorrect because your current estimate

904
00:52:25,610 --> 00:52:28,170
of the value function is going to have inevitably

905
00:52:28,170 --> 00:52:31,450
at least a small amount of error in it

906
00:52:31,450 --> 00:52:38,050
And so there might be a way to potentially balance this bias

907
00:52:38,050 --> 00:52:41,370
and variance a bit more

908
00:52:41,370 --> 00:52:43,730
And one way that we can think about this

909
00:52:43,730 --> 00:52:46,430
is if we have our trajectories, we

910
00:52:46,430 --> 00:52:47,990
can think of the Monte Carlo version

911
00:52:47,990 --> 00:52:50,830
as using the sum of rewards averaged

912
00:52:50,830 --> 00:52:54,270
over those trajectories, whereas the Tt version

913
00:52:54,270 --> 00:52:57,230
is going to be looking at the first reward

914
00:52:57,230 --> 00:52:59,810
plus the value right after that

915
00:52:59,810 --> 00:53:04,350
So it's r plus V for just like a temporal difference of just one

916
00:53:04,350 --> 00:53:06,750
time step

917
00:53:06,750 --> 00:53:10,330
And you can actually interpolate between these two estimates

918
00:53:10,330 --> 00:53:12,730
And instead of using just one time step

919
00:53:12,730 --> 00:53:14,370
you could use n time steps

920
00:53:14,370 --> 00:53:18,950
So you could look at sum of the rewards for n time

921
00:53:18,950 --> 00:53:22,390
steps in the future before the variance has increased

922
00:53:22,390 --> 00:53:26,630
substantially, and then use your value estimate after those n

923
00:53:26,630 --> 00:53:28,830
time steps

924
00:53:28,830 --> 00:53:32,830
And what this would look like is an equation like this

925
00:53:32,830 --> 00:53:36,790
where instead of just using one reward right here

926
00:53:36,790 --> 00:53:42,970
and instead of using all of the sum of rewards

927
00:53:42,970 --> 00:53:48,170
you can sum over the next n minus 1 rewards

928
00:53:48,170 --> 00:53:52,410
and then use your value estimate at t plus n

929
00:53:52,410 --> 00:53:59,370
And so this looks like a sum over from t to t

930
00:53:59,370 --> 00:54:10,450
plus n minus 1 r of S, a, plus your estimate of the value

931
00:54:10,450 --> 00:54:17,790
at t plus n

932
00:54:17,790 --> 00:54:19,250
In this case, this is going to have

933
00:54:19,250 --> 00:54:22,650
a lot less variance than the full Monte Carlo, especially

934
00:54:22,650 --> 00:54:25,650
if you have smaller n

935
00:54:25,650 --> 00:54:28,210
And it's going to be potentially less

936
00:54:28,210 --> 00:54:31,370
biased than just using a one step temporal difference

937
00:54:31,370 --> 00:54:33,450
bootstrap

938
00:54:33,450 --> 00:54:37,090
And so essentially you're going to have more information

939
00:54:37,090 --> 00:54:42,600
about the rewards that were directly observed

940
00:54:42,600 --> 00:54:44,420
So this can be written as this

941
00:54:44,420 --> 00:54:48,120
And in this case, this now becomes your target

942
00:54:48,120 --> 00:54:51,160
for supervised learning

943
00:54:51,160 --> 00:54:54,800
And yeah, this is often called n step returns

944
00:54:54,800 --> 00:54:58,360
because you're summing over n time steps

945
00:54:58,360 --> 00:55:05,320
In practice, the bootstrap version, if you set n equals t

946
00:55:05,320 --> 00:55:07,560
this is full Monte Carlo

947
00:55:07,560 --> 00:55:10,670
Oftentimes somewhere in between 1 and nt

948
00:55:10,670 --> 00:55:12,760
works the best in practice

949
00:55:12,760 --> 00:55:15,440
And this can be especially useful in scenarios

950
00:55:15,440 --> 00:55:17,440
where you have very small time steps

951
00:55:17,440 --> 00:55:19,240
because if your time step is, I don't know

952
00:55:19,240 --> 00:55:20,680
10 milliseconds or something like

953
00:55:20,680 --> 00:55:26,920
that, then very little will have happened in between those time

954
00:55:26,920 --> 00:55:28,120
steps

955
00:55:28,120 --> 00:55:30,820
If you have a very long time trajectory

956
00:55:30,820 --> 00:55:36,840
can you use both of these, you're asking

957
00:55:36,840 --> 00:55:44,300
Oh, yeah

958
00:55:44,300 --> 00:55:48,300
You could add all of these to your data set in principle

959
00:55:48,300 --> 00:55:55,020
I think that typically the high variance like labels

960
00:55:55,020 --> 00:55:56,420
the things that are high variance

961
00:55:56,420 --> 00:56:02,000
can deteriorate learning because it leads to more stochasticity

962
00:56:02,000 --> 00:56:05,960
And likewise, if your values are not very good

963
00:56:05,960 --> 00:56:08,520
then this can also be a challenge

964
00:56:08,520 --> 00:56:09,940
And so in practice, you'll usually

965
00:56:09,940 --> 00:56:14,660
just pick one of these versions and supervise with that

966
00:56:14,660 --> 00:56:18,160
In principle, you could combine them

967
00:56:18,160 --> 00:56:23,110
But yeah, in practice usually you don't

968
00:56:23,110 --> 00:56:24,960
One aside here

969
00:56:24,960 --> 00:56:28,160
So far we've been summing rewards

970
00:56:28,160 --> 00:56:31,340
And we actually talked in the first lecture

971
00:56:31,340 --> 00:56:34,460
about how, in some cases, we might not

972
00:56:34,460 --> 00:56:37,320
want to just blindly sum all rewards in the future

973
00:56:37,320 --> 00:56:39,800
And in particular, if you think about scenarios

974
00:56:39,800 --> 00:56:44,780
where the length of the episode is infinite or very

975
00:56:44,780 --> 00:56:49,240
very long, then your estimate of the value

976
00:56:49,240 --> 00:56:53,000
can get very large in those cases

977
00:56:53,000 --> 00:56:55,080
and so you might not want to just

978
00:56:55,080 --> 00:56:58,840
be summing over all of the rewards in the future

979
00:56:58,840 --> 00:57:02,200
And so a simple trick to handle the fact

980
00:57:02,200 --> 00:57:04,240
that your estimates of the value can get very big

981
00:57:04,240 --> 00:57:10,400
is to weight your objective, weight the sum of rewards

982
00:57:10,400 --> 00:57:14,280
to be biased towards the earlier rewards

983
00:57:14,280 --> 00:57:16,960
And the way that you can write this

984
00:57:16,960 --> 00:57:24,160
is, instead of having r plus V, you can add a coefficient right

985
00:57:24,160 --> 00:57:25,820
here

986
00:57:25,820 --> 00:57:28,560
If this coefficient is 1, it's the same as before

987
00:57:28,560 --> 00:57:32,140
But you can set it to something like 0.9 or 0.99

988
00:57:32,140 --> 00:57:33,640
And what this is going to do is it's

989
00:57:33,640 --> 00:57:36,080
going to say that I care more about the rewards

990
00:57:36,080 --> 00:57:39,540
at the current time step and less about the rewards

991
00:57:39,540 --> 00:57:47,100
at the future time steps

992
00:57:47,100 --> 00:57:50,100
Adding this discount factor that's

993
00:57:50,100 --> 00:57:52,180
discounting future rewards is actually

994
00:57:52,180 --> 00:57:56,660
can be viewed as changing the decision process slightly

995
00:57:56,660 --> 00:57:58,740
So it's actually equivalent to saying

996
00:57:58,740 --> 00:58:04,940
that there is some probability that the agent will

997
00:58:04,940 --> 00:58:07,460
die at any given point in time

998
00:58:07,460 --> 00:58:09,180
And so this is equivalent to saying

999
00:58:09,180 --> 00:58:13,660
that with some probability-- so say

1000
00:58:13,660 --> 00:58:21,140
gamma is like 0.99, in this case, using this form

1001
00:58:21,140 --> 00:58:23,060
is equivalent to saying that there's

1002
00:58:23,060 --> 00:58:26,660
a 1% chance that the agent will enter some state that has 0

1003
00:58:26,660 --> 00:58:31,620
reward and stay there forever

1004
00:58:31,620 --> 00:58:35,540
And so if you have some original MDP right here

1005
00:58:35,540 --> 00:58:38,610
the way that this discount factor would change

1006
00:58:38,610 --> 00:58:45,210
the MDP is it would say that with probability 1 minus gamma

1007
00:58:45,210 --> 00:58:50,330
you enter this death state where you can't

1008
00:58:50,330 --> 00:58:52,370
do anything else after that

1009
00:58:52,370 --> 00:58:57,570
And the original probabilities in the rest of the MDP

1010
00:58:57,570 --> 00:59:01,560
are all multiplied by gamma

1011
00:59:01,560 --> 00:59:03,890
The question is, is there any relationship between gamma

1012
00:59:03,890 --> 00:59:05,850
and these n step things

1013
00:59:05,850 --> 00:59:12,300
If gamma is small, would that change like what you do

1014
00:59:12,300 --> 00:59:14,130
The way that you would modify this objective

1015
00:59:14,130 --> 00:59:19,930
would be gamma to the n

1016
00:59:19,930 --> 00:59:22,000
Possibly, I mean, I think that they're all

1017
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:24,170
interconnected in the sense that the choice of gamma

1018
00:59:24,170 --> 00:59:26,310
might depend on the environment

1019
00:59:26,310 --> 00:59:30,510
And, generally, if going to be looking further into the future

1020
00:59:30,510 --> 00:59:32,930
that's because you're going to be in an environment that's

1021
00:59:32,930 --> 00:59:35,130
more deterministic or a smaller time steps

1022
00:59:35,130 --> 00:59:38,230
and that would also affect the choice of gamma

1023
00:59:38,230 --> 00:59:41,430
The question was, in terms of choosing how far you look ahead

1024
00:59:41,430 --> 00:59:43,510
does that affect whether you're prioritizing short

1025
00:59:43,510 --> 00:59:45,670
versus long term

1026
00:59:45,670 --> 00:59:49,750
In this case, it is more a factor of--

1027
00:59:49,750 --> 00:59:52,950
so it doesn't actually affect how greedy

1028
00:59:52,950 --> 00:59:56,070
you are because the value function is representing

1029
00:59:56,070 --> 00:59:57,130
the sum of rewards

1030
00:59:57,130 --> 00:59:59,950
And so these are still estimating

1031
00:59:59,950 --> 01:00:02,390
the same exact quantity

1032
01:00:02,390 --> 01:00:05,790
It's more a factor of how far in the future

1033
01:00:05,790 --> 01:00:07,930
are you looking ahead in terms of the rewards

1034
01:00:07,930 --> 01:00:11,570
And it's more a factor of how much is the variance increasing

1035
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:15,170
And if the variance increases somewhat slowly

1036
01:00:15,170 --> 01:00:17,830
then you can look further ahead into the future

1037
01:00:17,830 --> 01:00:19,990
If it increases very, very quickly, then you

1038
01:00:19,990 --> 01:00:25,660
might want to use a smaller n

1039
01:00:25,660 --> 01:00:27,530
So let's walk through the full algorithm now

1040
01:00:27,530 --> 01:00:31,310
So we talked about estimating value functions, now let's

1041
01:00:31,310 --> 01:00:33,130
actually put this into practice

1042
01:00:33,130 --> 01:00:35,730
So what we can do is we can sample a batch of data

1043
01:00:35,730 --> 01:00:40,170
from our policy, then we will fit our value estimate

1044
01:00:40,170 --> 01:00:42,850
to the summed rewards in the data

1045
01:00:42,850 --> 01:00:48,330
And we can do this by looking at the rewards for a short period

1046
01:00:48,330 --> 01:00:50,050
of time and then the value

1047
01:00:50,050 --> 01:00:53,130
So this would be the equation for n step returns

1048
01:00:53,130 --> 01:00:55,890
And then when we fit this we'll run multiple gradient

1049
01:00:55,890 --> 01:00:58,690
steps on this objective that's trying to minimize

1050
01:00:58,690 --> 01:01:00,370
the difference between our estimates

1051
01:01:00,370 --> 01:01:04,770
and the observed future rewards

1052
01:01:04,770 --> 01:01:07,070
Then once we have our estimate of the value

1053
01:01:07,070 --> 01:01:09,890
then we can use that to estimate the advantage

1054
01:01:09,890 --> 01:01:11,850
of a particular action by looking

1055
01:01:11,850 --> 01:01:14,690
at the reward plus the value at the next time

1056
01:01:14,690 --> 01:01:17,470
step minus the value at the current time step

1057
01:01:17,470 --> 01:01:20,490
So this is telling us according to our value function

1058
01:01:20,490 --> 01:01:24,410
and the reward we saw, how good is the action that

1059
01:01:24,410 --> 01:01:27,210
was taken in the data set

1060
01:01:27,210 --> 01:01:30,490
And then we can use this to get a gradient for our policy

1061
01:01:30,490 --> 01:01:34,470
where we will be increasing the likelihood of actions that

1062
01:01:34,470 --> 01:01:36,530
are estimated to be advantageous

1063
01:01:36,530 --> 01:01:38,310
and decreasing the likelihood for actions

1064
01:01:38,310 --> 01:01:41,310
that are not advantageous

1065
01:01:41,310 --> 01:01:43,950
And then lastly, we update our policy

1066
01:01:43,950 --> 01:01:48,650
by applying this gradient step to our policy parameters

1067
01:01:48,650 --> 01:01:50,110
and then we can repeat this process

1068
01:01:50,110 --> 01:01:55,390
to then sample a new batch of data for our policy

1069
01:01:55,390 --> 01:01:57,450
Now, note here we have two neural networks

1070
01:01:57,450 --> 01:02:00,150
One is for the policy and one is for the value function

1071
01:02:00,150 --> 01:02:01,830
This is called an actor-critic algorithm

1072
01:02:01,830 --> 01:02:04,870
because one of the networks is the actor, one of the networks

1073
01:02:04,870 --> 01:02:06,750
is the value function, which can also

1074
01:02:06,750 --> 01:02:10,030
be called a critic because it's criticizing

1075
01:02:10,030 --> 01:02:16,950
how good a state and policy is, and that's our algorithm

1076
01:02:16,950 --> 01:02:18,990
And coming back to the examples that we

1077
01:02:18,990 --> 01:02:20,630
saw before, this is something that's

1078
01:02:20,630 --> 01:02:22,070
able to actually make use of data

1079
01:02:22,070 --> 01:02:24,030
for example, that takes a step forward and then

1080
01:02:24,030 --> 01:02:27,610
falls backwards because you can actually learn from the data

1081
01:02:27,610 --> 01:02:29,670
especially when you use bootstrapped estimates

1082
01:02:29,670 --> 01:02:33,500
that taking a step forward is advantageous and has

1083
01:02:33,500 --> 01:02:36,540
a positive advantage compared to other actions

1084
01:02:36,540 --> 01:02:37,450
that you might take

1085
01:02:37,450 --> 01:02:39,700
And this is going to be making much more efficient use

1086
01:02:39,700 --> 01:02:45,430
of the data that we've collected

1087
01:02:45,430 --> 01:02:46,140
So review so far

1088
01:02:46,140 --> 01:02:47,580
We've talked about policy gradients, which

1089
01:02:47,580 --> 01:02:50,120
is trying to do more of the good stuff, less of the bad stuff

1090
01:02:50,120 --> 01:02:51,460
based on what you observe

1091
01:02:51,460 --> 01:02:54,440
Actor-critic are learning to estimate what's good and bad

1092
01:02:54,440 --> 01:02:55,980
and then doing more of the good stuff

1093
01:02:55,980 --> 01:02:59,220
according to your estimate

1094
01:02:59,220 --> 01:03:02,900
And we can get a better estimate by using a neural network

1095
01:03:02,900 --> 01:03:06,660
to try to explicitly estimate the value function

1096
01:03:06,660 --> 01:03:08,580
And then we introduced this third step

1097
01:03:08,580 --> 01:03:11,540
where we are fitting this model as part of the improvement

1098
01:03:11,540 --> 01:03:13,200
process

1099
01:03:13,200 --> 01:03:14,700
We talked about three different ways

1100
01:03:14,700 --> 01:03:16,580
to estimate value, either directly looking

1101
01:03:16,580 --> 01:03:19,800
at some rewards, bootstrapping off the value function

1102
01:03:19,800 --> 01:03:21,380
and this hybrid

1103
01:03:21,380 --> 01:03:25,770
And this is often referred to as policy evaluation

